<!-- ============================================================ OVERVIEW -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-label">Section 1</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Since the 6th Academy Awards ceremony in 1934, Hollywood's highest honor for film composition has been awarded across 93 unbroken ceremonies. From Louis Silvers' pioneering win for <em>One Night of Love</em> to Ludwig Göransson's landmark fourth victory for <em>Sinners</em> in 2026, the Best Original Score Oscar has charted the full arc of cinematic music — from sweeping orchestral Romanticism through jazz-inflected cool, synth experimentation, and the genre-fusing plurality of the contemporary era.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro" style="margin-top: -24px;">
      This report draws on a complete, verified dataset of all 93 winners across three analysis dimensions: <strong>Year</strong>, <strong>Film</strong>, and <strong>Composer</strong>. Because the dataset includes several multi-composer shared wins, the total individual composer count (79) exceeds the number of unique composer-credit entries. Note that a small number of years (e.g., 1939, 1941, 1942) saw shared wins by large consortia of composers, reflecting the Academy's early practices around studio music departments. All statistics reported here are derived directly from the dataset with no imputation or estimation.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Category history note:</strong> The Best Original Score category has evolved through multiple name changes and occasional splits (e.g., separate awards for Dramatic Score and Comedy/Musical Score in certain periods). This dataset presents one consolidated winner per ceremony year — the definitive single award recognized in each year's ceremony.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

