<section class="section" id="era">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-label">Section 4</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Dividing 93 ceremonies into four approximate eras reveals distinct aesthetic regimes — each defined by its dominant composers, characteristic musical languages, and the kinds of films the Academy elevated. The boundaries are historiographic approximations derived from the Year column, as no era labels are present in the source data.
    </p>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card golden">
        <div class="era-name">Golden Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1934 – 1959 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 26 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">26</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Composers</span><span class="era-stat-value">22</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">~35%</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Top Repeat Winner</span><span class="era-stat-value">Max Steiner (3×)</span></div>
        <div class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Alfred Newman, Erich Wolfgang Korngold, Miklos Rozsa, Franz Waxman.<br><br>
          The Golden Age was the studio orchestra at its most imperial — full string sections, sweeping leitmotifs, and scores that functioned as emotional scaffolding for genre epics. European émigrés (Korngold, Rozsa, Steiner) dominated, bringing Viennese late-Romantic traditions to Hollywood. Biennial domination by a single composer (Tiomkin in 1952, 1954, 1958) was common.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card silver">
        <div class="era-name">Silver Age</div>
        <div class="era-years">1960 – 1979 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 20 Ceremonies</div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Ceremonies</span><span class="era-stat-value">20</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Unique Composers</span><span class="era-stat-value">19</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Repeat Win Rate</span><span class="era-stat-value">~20%</span></div>
        <div class="era-stat"><span class="era-stat-label">Top Repeat Winner</span><span class="era-stat-value">Maurice Jarre (2×), John Barry (2×)</span></div>
        <div class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> Maurice Jarre, John Barry, Henry Mancini, Nino Rota, John Williams (debut).<br><br>
          The Silver Age brought greater diversity: jazz inflections (Mancini, Bacharach), continental cool (Lai, Legrand, Moroder), and the emergence of pop-crossover sensibilities. The epic orchestral tradition persisted through Jarre's Lawrence of Arabia (1962) and Doctor Zhivago (1965), but by the late 1970s, John Williams's blockbuster symphonics (Jaws, Star Wars) announced a new era's arrival.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="era-card modern">
        <div class="era-name">Modern Era</div>
        <div class="era-years">1980 – 1999 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 20 Ceremonies</div>
